WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.350
Over a century after he first appeared in
movies, Charlie Chaplin’s name still conjures

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.380
images of a funny little guy in a bowler hat
causing mirth-inducing chaos.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.680
By some measures, his Little Tramp is the
most famous character to have ever existed,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.910
a clown so beloved that not even Hitler stealing
his mustache could ruin him for us.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.980
But what about the man behind the character?

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.810
Born into grinding poverty in Victorian London,
Charlie Chaplin rose from the gutter to become

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.970
the highest paid actor in Hollywood.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.509
He met with icons.

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:32.970
Courted revolutionaries.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.650
And then lived long enough to see his reputation
dragged through the mud and his adopted country

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.650
disown him.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.450
They say that behind the smile of every clown
lurks tears.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.330
If that’s true, then it makes sense that
the story of cinema’s greatest clown would

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.270
be the most tragic of all.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:01:02.420
The Kid (1889 – 1908)
When Charles Spencer Chaplin was born in London

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.369
on April 16, 1889, it must have seemed like
he’d hit the artistic jackpot.

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:12.079
His mother, Hannah, was a singer, while his
father, Charles Chaplin Sr., was an up-and-coming

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:13.079
comic.

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:17.380
Barely a year after young Charlie had been
born, Charles Sr. was touring the USA and

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.060
pulling in megabucks.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.170
Unfortunately, he was doing it without Charlie
or Hannah.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:29.659
That spring, 1890, Charles Sr. had walked
out on his wife and baby son and taken his

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:30.670
money with him.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:35.850
Hannah, Charlie, and Charlie’s older half-brother
Sydney were plunged into biting poverty.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.819
This being Victorian England, biting poverty
really did bite.

00:01:39.819 --> 00:01:40.819
Hard.

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:44.350
Still, Hannah did what she could for her boys,
providing for them with her singing work,

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.109
teaching them her routines.

00:01:46.109 --> 00:01:50.499
Much later, Charlie would say his own skill
as a dancer, singer and mimic came from observing

00:01:50.499 --> 00:01:52.320
Hannah as she did the housework.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.359
By the age of five, young Charlie was able
to mimic his mother’s most popular songs.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:02:00.079
This turned out to be super useful when, one
evening in 1894, Hannah’s voice gave out

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:01.649
halfway through a performance.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:03.439
Charlie was watching from the wings.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:07.850
When Hannah stopped singing, the manager simply
shoved the child onstage and told him to do

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:08.850
something!

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.080
So Charlie did.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.409
In front of a cheering crowd, he finished
his mother’s ditty for her, bringing the

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:16.409
house down.

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:20.650
In any other biography, this would be the
heartwarming moment Charlie attained stardom.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.879
Sadly, there’s very little that’s heartwarming
about the life of Charlie Chaplin.

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:30.459
Hannah’s lost voice wasn’t laryngitis,
but the first outward sign of the mental illness

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:31.790
that was consuming her.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.409
On June 29, 1895, she was committed to an
insane asylum.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:39.700
Too young to fend for themselves, Charlie
and Sydney went to the workhouse.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.720
The tales of their next few years are heartbreaking.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.880
At some point they went to live with Charlie’s
father, Charles Sr., but the abuse they suffered

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.330
at his alcoholic hands was so great the authorities
were forced to step in.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.720
Not that Charles Sr. didn’t sometimes try
to act the father.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.310
In 1897, he got Charlie a gig with a clog
dancing troupe known as the Eight Lancashire

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.310
Lads.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.370
But the troupe soon dissolved, and Charlie
went straight back to the workhouse.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.260
On May 9, 1901, Charles Chaplin Sr. finally
died of alcohol related illnesses.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.230
Not long after, Hannah – herself only just
back in the boys’ lives – had a final

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.880
relapse and was recommitted to the asylum.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.569
This time, young Charlie was completely alone.

00:03:18.569 --> 00:03:22.290
Sydney had taken the only way out of poverty
he could and joined the Navy.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:26.069
With his mother, brother, and father gone,
Charlie struggled to survive.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:30.209
Over the next five years, Charlie worked any
job he could get his hands on.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:34.140
Newspaper vendor, toymaker, printer, doctor’s
boy… whatever paid, he’d do it.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.500
When he couldn’t get work, he simply resorted
to sleeping rough.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.989
Although he picked up acting parts on the
side, including a one year run in a 1903 stage

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.780
production of Sherlock Holmes, they were never
enough to support his dream of becoming an

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.780
actor.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.500
That finally changed in 1908.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.519
That year Charlie Chaplin, by then a lad of
19, landed a gig with the Fred Karno Repertoire

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:56.519
Company.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:00.700
Although the work was minor – he played
“the Drunk” in a sketch – it brought

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.609
him into contact with the right people.

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:04.739
Stan Laurel, for instance, was another cast
member.

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:08.569
Perhaps more important still, in 1910, the
Fred Karno company gave Charlie the chance

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:10.819
to do something he’d never dreamed of.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:19.259
He was going to tour America.

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.579
The Tramp (1910 – 1918)
In fall, 1912, American movie producer Mack

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:25.639
Senett was looking to add new talent to his
burgeoning stock of actors.

00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:29.660
The head of Keystone Films, Senett was one
of the pioneers cashing in on the new gold

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.180
rush in California.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:36.280
Silent films were a fresh, exciting medium
in 1912, where money could be quickly made.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.990
Provided, of course, you could find the comic
talent.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:43.180
That year, one of the best reviewed comics
in America was an English lad called Charlie

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.040
Chaplin, on a second tour with Fred Karno’s
company.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.069
So Senett set about luring him to Keystone.

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:50.390
He offered him $150 a week.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.410
The chance of continuous work.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:57.479
Chaplin dithered for an entire year before
finally accepting Senett’s terms in November,

00:04:57.479 --> 00:04:58.479
1913.

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:01.620
Not two weeks later, he was Keystone Studios’
newest actor.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.310
Making a Living, the first Charlie Chaplin
film in cinematic history debuted on February

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.800
2, 1914.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.740
It was… not a success.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:15.199
Chaplin and the director, Henry Lehrman, fell
out so badly that Lehrman cut most of Chaplin’s

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:16.550
gags from the final edit.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:20.259
Even had he kept them, it’s unlikely Making
a Living would be fondly remembered.

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:22.430
Chaplin’s character was nasty.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:23.430
Unfunny.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:28.700
Perhaps Keystone had made a bad investment
in its new comedian?

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:33.250
If the thought ever flashed through Mack Senett’s
mind, it was soon gone.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.389
After Making a Living wrapped, Senett was
at the Keystone lot, watching the filming

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:39.040
of a short called Mabel’s Strange Predicament.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.449
Chaplin was hanging around, hoping to be noticed,
when Senett said “we need some gags here.”

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:45.420
The producer pointed at Chaplin.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.560
“Put on comedy makeup.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.560
Anything will do.”

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.110
Chaplin went to the prop department, unsure
what Senett wanted.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.639
There, he found a pair of baggy trousers,
a bowler hat, and a cane.

00:05:54.639 --> 00:05:56.940
As Chaplin later said:
“I had no idea of the character.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.080
But the moment I was dressed, the clothes
and the makeup made me feel the person he

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.080
was.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.220
I began to know him, and by the time I walked
on to the stage he was fully born.”

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.240
That “he” was the Little Tramp.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.440
And he was about to make Chaplin’s fortune.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.009
In the Tramp costume, Chaplin made the crew
on Mabel’s Strange Predicament laugh so

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:19.789
hard the entire Keystone company stopped to
watch.

00:06:19.789 --> 00:06:24.840
By the end of the day, Chaplin swore he’d
never perform without his new costume again.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:30.590
And so it was that on February 7, 1914, the
Tramp’s second Keystone picture, Kid Auto

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.960
Races at Venice, beat Mabel’s Strange Predicament
into theaters.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.610
It was an instant hit.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.160
You can clearly see the effect the Tramp had
on Chaplin’s career.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.789
Over 1914, Chaplin made 32 more films for
Keystone.

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:47.080
When his contract ran out in December, a bidding
war erupted that made Senett’s initial offer

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.000
of $150 a week look pitiful.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.990
Essanay Studios offered Chaplin $1,250 a week
plus a $10,000 signing bonus.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.490
Not being an idiot, Chaplin said yes.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:01.710
The next few years saw Chaplin’s star go
supernova.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:07.069
At Essanay, he rebooted the Little Tramp in
1915’s The Tramp, making the character more

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:08.080
sweet natured.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.330
The new approach was so successful that Mutual
Films poached Chaplin from Essanay with an

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.819
offer of $10,000 a week.

00:07:14.819 --> 00:07:18.720
Aged 26, Chaplin was now the highest paid
actor in Hollywood.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.050
Over the next two years, Chaplin shot 12 shorts
for Mutual.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.039
He later called those years the happiest of
his life.

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:27.110
Not that happiness stopped him from moving
on.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.479
At the end of 1917, Chaplin terminated his
Mutual contact.

00:07:30.479 --> 00:07:34.650
He built his own studio, signing a deal with
First National to release his pictures without

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:35.650
interference.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:40.289
Not yet 30, Chaplin was now the most famous
movie star in America.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:41.710
His next move?

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:49.340
To conquer the world.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.590
The Gold Rush (1918 – 1928)
The next decade was a golden time for Chaplin

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:52.590
the filmmaker.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.080
His first feature, The Kid, came out in 1920.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.300
The Gold Rush, possibly his best work, followed
in 1924.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:03.539
In 1928 he made The Circus, a film Orson Welles
– the director of Citizen Kane, the greatest

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:07.650
movie of all time – once called “the greatest
movie of all time.”

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.349
It was also a golden time for Chaplin the
producer.

00:08:10.349 --> 00:08:16.889
In 1919, he ditched First National and cofounded
United Artists with Mary Pickford and DW Griffith,

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:19.729
giving them total control over their pictures.

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:24.060
So… that’s it for the rest of this video,
huh?

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:30.280
Us throwing movie names at you and detailing
just how exceptionally rich and famous Chaplin

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.290
became.

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.320
Not quite.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:38.120
If the decade following 1918 was a golden
time for Chaplin’s public faces, it was

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.370
far less so for his private one.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.640
It was in this period that the darkly flawed
Chaplin emerged.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.959
It began in fall 1918, when Mildred Harris
told Chaplin she was pregnant.

00:08:48.959 --> 00:08:52.240
The two had started seeing each other earlier
in the year in secret.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.240
In secret because, while Chaplin was nearly
30, Harris was barely half his age.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:01.530
Under California law, having sex with someone
aged only 16 was statutory rape.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:06.550
So, when Harris told Chaplin she was pregnant,
it sent the star into panic mode.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.290
That October, 1918, Chaplin married Harris
in a secret ceremony.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.600
Not long after, Harris’s pregnancy vanished.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.500
Suspecting his new wife had tricked him, Chaplin
resolved to make her life hell.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.930
Over the next two years, Chaplin badly mistreated
Harris.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.190
Although he was never physical, he was mentally
abusive, ignoring her, belittling her, calling

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.190
her an idiot.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:31.380
In 1920, when Harris was 18, Chaplin finally
got her pregnant for real then divorced her.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.480
It wouldn’t be his only icky behavior that
decade.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:39.240
The same year he divorced Harris, Chaplin
met Lita Gray, then a mere 12 years old.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.870
Chaplin kept her close for the next few years,
until Gray finally turned either 15 or 16,

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.960
depending on which source you read, then seduced
her.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.760
Today, we would call this behavior “grooming”.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.800
Even in the freewheeling ‘20s, it was shocking.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.290
On November 23, 1924, Lita told Chaplin she
was pregnant.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:02.120
Once again hasty to avoid a statutory rape
charge, Chaplin married her in New Mexico.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.730
Once again, the Little Tramp made their marriage
a misery.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:09.470
Nor was it just Chaplin’s home life that
was suddenly devoid of laughter.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:14.440
The same month he married Lita, Chaplin was
on a yacht when producer Thomas Ince died

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:15.800
in mysterious circumstances.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.510
It’s long been rumored that Chaplin was
sleeping with newspaper baron William Randolph

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:24.730
Hearst’s mistress, and that Hearst accidentally
shot Ince while trying to kill Chaplin.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:30.060
But if the rumors were bad enough, the divorce
suit Lita filed in 1927 was even worse.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.480
After two miserable years with Chaplin, Lita
was a broken woman.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.820
In her court filing she accused Chaplin of
trying to force an abortion onto her, of mental

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.040
cruelty, and of forcing her to participate
in sex acts so disgusting there’s no way

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.920
we can realistically describe them here without
getting our entire channel banned.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:52.490
Although Chaplin settled the suit out of court
for $600,000, the scandal consumed him.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.820
Chaplin was used to being a household name.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.490
Now he was a lightning rod for those who hated
the loose morals of the age.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:01.610
Still, the 1920s ended on a high.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.490
The Circus was a major success, and Chaplin
quickly began work on the film that would

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.370
become City Lights.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.649
By now, though, storm clouds were gathering.

00:11:09.649 --> 00:11:13.440
Two events were about to happen that would
shake up Chaplin’s world.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:21.889
One in Wall Street, the other in the very
town that had made his fortune.

00:11:21.889 --> 00:11:26.769
Modern Times (1929-1942)
On October 6, 1927, a little film called The

00:11:26.769 --> 00:11:28.269
Jazz Singer premiered.

00:11:28.269 --> 00:11:31.970
The tale of a white Jewish man who blacks
up to becomes a jazz legend, the film would

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:37.110
probably be forgotten today, were it not for
one major technical innovation.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:41.920
The Jazz Singer was the first feature film
in American history to feature a synchronized

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:42.920
soundtrack.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:45.340
The age of talkies had finally arrived.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:49.200
Famously, silent stars like Chaplin were dismissive
of the new technology.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.709
While preparing City Lights, Chaplin claimed
the new fad wouldn’t last six months.

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:56.949
There were no such delusions over the next
world shakeup.

00:11:56.949 --> 00:12:01.079
Two years after The Jazz Singer premiered,
the New York stock market gave a final sigh

00:12:01.079 --> 00:12:05.110
and collapsed into a pile of broken American
dreams.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:09.480
The wreckage of that fateful day would become
the Great Depression, and it affected everyone.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:14.110
But while the advent of talkies was ruinous
for most silent stars, the Great Depression

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:16.610
gave Chaplin’s work a sudden urgency.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:22.050
For Jazz Age audiences, a Tramp with a heart
of gold was as exotic as superheroes are to

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:23.050
us today.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.660
For filmgoers in the 30s, though, he was the
exact tonic they needed.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:31.980
Despite being a silent anachronism when it
appeared in 1931, City Lights was a standout

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:32.980
success.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:34.760
Not that Chaplin enjoyed the accolade.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:39.660
Seemingly depressed by the way the world and
his art form were going, he left the States

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.100
in 1931 and embarked on a world tour.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:46.130
During this time, the actor met everyone from
Albert Einstein to Mahatma Gandhi.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:49.860
But it was what Chaplin witnessed in Europe
that concerns us today.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:54.300
The rise of automation and nationalism was
fraying the continent at the edges.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:58.920
When Chaplin returned to Hollywood in July
1932, he was obsessed with doing something

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.000
about these twin evils.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.440
On one level, that meant starting work on
Modern Times, his 1936 satire of capitalism.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:09.050
On another, it meant leaning towards politics
for the first time in his life.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:13.089
It meant becoming openly sympathetic to Communism.

00:13:13.089 --> 00:13:15.899
This radical shift did not go unnoticed.

00:13:15.899 --> 00:13:20.110
In 1939, Chaplin began shooting his first
real talkie: The Great Dictator.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:25.550
A satire of nationalism in general and Adolf
Hitler in particular, it was made at a time

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.380
when the US was officially neutral in the
war engulfing Europe.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.140
So when the film premiered in 1940 with its
political ending speech, audiences didn’t

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:36.320
see the Little Tramp exhorting the end of
a horrific war.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.209
They saw a suspected Communist trying to ram
leftwing ideology down their throats.

00:13:40.209 --> 00:13:43.769
Just one year before, in 1938, the House Un-American
Activities Committee (HUAC for short) had

00:13:43.769 --> 00:13:47.420
come into existence following a report that
Hollywood was teeming with Reds.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:50.560
Now, Chaplin was confirming their worst suspicions.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.639
And, boy, did he ever confirm them.

00:13:53.639 --> 00:13:58.079
After the attack on Pearl Harbor dragged the
US into WWII, Chaplin became a professional

00:13:58.079 --> 00:13:59.450
agitator for the Red Army.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:04.160
At an ill-advised speech in New York, he called
Stalin’s purges “a wonderful thing,”

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.839
and followed up by saying, “the only people
who object to Communism… are the Nazi agents

00:14:08.839 --> 00:14:09.910
in this country.”

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:14.459
In the context of a war in which the US was
allied with the USSR, these comments may have

00:14:14.459 --> 00:14:15.459
seemed justifiable.

00:14:15.459 --> 00:14:20.779
But for those who already suspected Chaplin
of anti-Americanism, this was fuel for the

00:14:20.779 --> 00:14:21.779
fire.

00:14:21.779 --> 00:14:26.290
By late 1942, prominent media personalities
were questioning Chaplin’s commitment to

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:27.290
America.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:32.690
All it would take would be one giant anti-Communist
panic and his reputation could be utterly

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:33.690
destroyed.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:34.690
But this was the early 1940s.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:39.330
There was no way America could just be a few
short years away from being consumed in a

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:40.470
Red scare.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.060
Could there?

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:49.680
Monsieur Verdoux (1943 – 1952)
April 11, 1947, is the day Charlie Chaplin

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.290
lit the fuse on the bomb that destroyed his
career.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.420
That day, he released his movie Monsieur Verdoux.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:00.240
A black comedy featuring Chaplin as a serial
killer who marries and murders old ladies

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.680
for their money, Monsieur Verdoux almost bankrupted
United Artists.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:08.790
It led to Chaplin being blacklisted and nearly
indicted by HUAC.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:12.380
Today, the film is regarded as a late Chaplin
classic.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:13.449
But in 1947?

00:15:13.449 --> 00:15:17.839
It was the equivalent of watching Mr. Rogers
kick a puppy to death while wearing a swastika.

00:15:17.839 --> 00:15:24.300
To understand just how Monsieur Verdoux came
to annihilate everything Chaplin held dear,

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.699
we need to go back in time to 1941.

00:15:26.699 --> 00:15:30.790
As you’ll probably remember, this was when
Chaplin was just starting to mouth off about

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:32.610
how great Stalinism was.

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.839
It was also when he met a young actress named
Joan Barry.

00:15:35.839 --> 00:15:40.430
Barry was slightly older than Chaplin’s
previous conquests, being the grand old age

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.440
of 21 when the 42 year old Chaplin met her.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.709
They started an affair, but it wasn’t long
before things got out of hand.

00:15:47.709 --> 00:15:51.730
In 1943, Barry sued Chaplin for impregnating
and abandoning her.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:56.089
Unfortunately for Chaplin, prosecutors decided
to hit him with everything they had.

00:15:56.089 --> 00:16:00.910
Chaplin was charged under the 1910 Mann Act,
better known as the White Slave Traffic Act.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:05.090
Designed to stop people taking prostitutes
over state lines, it wound up being so broadly

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:09.700
worded that anyone who brought someone to
another state even for consensual sex could

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:10.700
be charged.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.579
That was exactly what Chaplin had done.

00:16:13.579 --> 00:16:15.630
The trial was a media circus.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:20.160
The phrase “Charlie Chaplin on trial for
white slavery” was simply too irresistible.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:25.630
While Chaplin was acquitted, the smoke now
billowing around him convinced many there

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:29.690
was a dark fire burning behind his public
persona.

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:31.690
The thing is, they weren’t wrong.

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:35.470
We’ve already heard how atrociously Chaplin
treated the women in his life.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:37.690
And he really was sympathetic to Communism.

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:42.300
It also didn’t help that during his trial
he’d started an affair with another 18-year

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.560
old, Oona O’Neill.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.829
But the Barry trial turned everyone on him.

00:16:47.829 --> 00:16:52.830
Notorious columnist Hedda Hopper joined forces
with FBI Director J. Edgar Hoover to destroy

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:53.970
the Little Tramp.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:58.009
In the Senate, William Langer of North Dakota
started advocating for Chaplin’s deportation.

00:16:58.009 --> 00:17:01.980
As 1947 dawned, Chaplin was in dire need of
a comeback.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.010
United Artists was nearly out of money.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:06.060
His reputation was in ruins.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:11.390
What was needed was another City Lights, a
film to remind the public why they loved him.

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:15.360
Instead, the public got Monsieur Verdoux,
a film that ends with Chaplin comparing the

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:20.790
capitalist system to mass murder and implicating
every single American in war crimes for the

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:22.590
atom bombing of Hiroshima.

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:24.090
It was the last straw.

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:28.490
In Washington, HUAC informed Chaplin he would
likely be subpoenaed.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:32.630
While he never was, the McCarthyites still
destroyed him.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:37.020
That November, 1947, the Hollywood Ten were
jailed for refusing to name and shame other

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:38.020
Communists.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:42.540
In the wake of the scandal, 300 more stars
were blacklisted, including Chaplin.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:47.910
Still, Chaplin was Chaplin, which meant he
had money and influence at his disposal that

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:49.520
other blacklisted stars didn’t.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:54.200
Even while on the blacklist, he found the
money to crank out Limelight, a part-schmaltzy,

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:58.220
part-touching picture about an ageing clown
whose glory days are over.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:03.130
Scheduled for a September, 1952 release, Chaplin
decided to travel to Europe to drum up publicity.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:07.620
On September 18, Chaplin and his now-wife
Oona boarded a ship headed for England.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:11.360
They wouldn’t see America again for two
decades.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.740
The next day, the Attorney General cancelled
Chaplin’s reentry visa.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:18.460
Despite being in the US for forty years, the
star had never thought to trade his British

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.460
passport for an American one.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:23.600
And now America had blocked him from returning.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:28.490
When Limelight was released in the US, it
was picketed by morality crusaders and anti-Communists.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:33.210
No longer welcome in his adopted home, hated
by the America that had once embraced him,

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.430
Chaplin was suddenly as homeless as the Little
Tramp.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:45.540
His days as the greatest star in Hollywood
were over.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:47.101
The Freak (1953 – 1978)
The rest of Charlie Chaplin’s life can be

00:18:47.101 --> 00:18:53.400
easily divided into two chapters: the one
where everyone hated him, and the one where

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:56.020
they finally accepted him too late.

00:18:56.020 --> 00:19:01.330
After settling in Switzerland with Oona in
January 1953, Chaplin slowly started working

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.030
his way into the European film system.

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:08.600
In 1957, he released A King in New York, about
a king who flees into asylum in the USA and

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:11.450
suffers much of the same persecution that
Chaplin did.

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:16.010
Although the script went out of its way to
portray Americans as fundamentally decent,

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:17.260
America wasn’t listening.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.780
The movie was banned in most states for being
“unamerican”.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.710
This disappointment more or less set the stage
for the next decade.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:28.080
At one point, Chaplin considered reviving
the Little Tramp as the lone survivor of a

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:33.520
nuclear holocaust, only to instead make 1967’s
A Countess from Hong Kong, a film in which

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:38.940
the only gag seems to be Marlon Brando repeatedly
being caught with a nubile young woman in

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:39.940
his bedroom.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:43.160
It probably didn’t help that Chaplin’s
health was failing.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:47.430
After a series of strokes across the ‘60s,
he’d been left partially disabled.

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:51.350
It was in this sorry state that he started
work on his final project.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.320
The Freak was to be Chaplin’s coda.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:58.050
The story of a South American girl who grows
angel wings and becomes hunted and persecuted,

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.690
it was intended to cap his life’s work.

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:02.420
Sadly, it never got made.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:05.950
As the ‘60s became the ‘70s, Chaplin suffered
yet more strokes.

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:09.510
By 1974 he was wheelchair bound and in need
of care.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:13.280
Although he shot some test footage for The
Freak, everyone working with him knew the

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:16.580
director wouldn’t survive long enough to
get his picture made.

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:22.320
On Christmas Day, 1977, Charlie Chaplin suffered
a final, cataclysmic stroke in his sleep and

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:24.190
died, aged 88.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:27.640
Despite a brief return to the States five
years earlier to collect an honorary Academy

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:29.750
Award, he was still living in exile.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:33.140
Still sure America would never truly want
him back.

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:36.980
Normally this would be the point where we
concluded with a quick overview of Chaplin’s

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:40.730
life, to try and make some sense of his historical
standing.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:41.730
Not this time.

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:46.810
Because fate had one last, ghoulish twist
in store for the Little Tramp.

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:52.400
In March, 1978, two refugees from Communist
Eastern Europe dug up Chaplin’s corpse and

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:53.760
took it hostage.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:56.910
For the next 11 weeks, Chaplin’s body remained
missing.

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:02.310
Finally, in late May, 1978, Swiss police observed
one of the graverobbers trying to call Oona

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.000
with a ransom demand.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.900
He was arrested, and Chaplin’s missing body
located.

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:10.540
He was quickly reinterred, this time under
a concrete slab.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:15.520
Awful as this story is, we can’t help thinking
that Chaplin himself may have seen the potential

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:16.520
in it.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.250
The two graverobbers turned out to be dispossessed
immigrants living on the breadline, who wanted

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:22.970
to make some quick cash to support their families.

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:27.380
While the Little Tramp never did anything
as desperate as they did, in most other respects

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:29.460
his life wasn’t too dissimilar.

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:33.770
Maybe we’ve just been researching this video
too long, but you can almost imagine a darker

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:36.630
version of Chaplin making this film.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:41.700
Almost picture the Tramp’s oblivious face
as he’s accidentally roped into graverobbing,

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.950
lured by the promise of money for food.

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:45.990
In a way, maybe that’s fitting.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.960
Chaplin’s films were some of the very first
that truly sympathized with the poor, with

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:52.370
their struggles, their hopes and dreams.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:56.670
Using the magic of cinema, he conjured stories
from their hardscrabble lives… and turned

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:00.060
them into masterpieces not just of comedy,
but empathy too.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:05.800
He may have sometimes been cruel, sometimes
foolish, sometimes simply a raging egomaniac.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:09.860
But Charlie Chaplin was also the greatest
clown who ever lived.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:27.250
Tragic as his life was, he succeeded in bringing
joy

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:37.020
to millions.

